---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/back-in-stock';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/back-in-stock');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/back-in-stock');
const candidates = ['sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'privy', 'omnisend', 'firepush', 'drip', 'postscript', 'yotpo-email-sms', 'shopify-email', 'rejoiner'].map(s => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><article class="prose-shopify card mt-6">
<h1>Shopify SMS restock alerts: match the variant and pace the queue</h1>
<p>A merchandising team needs to alert people who requested a specific unavailable item without promising stock it cannot fulfill. The useful unit is the variant and the customer's request, not a generic marketing segment. A restock alert should explain availability; it should not imply a reservation unless inventory is actually reserved.</p>
<h2>Capture a request you can fulfill</h2><p>Store the product and variant identifiers, selected size or shade, signup time, channel requested and permission evidence. Keep restock requests distinct from general promotional subscriptions. Show how to leave the waitlist. On a product page with a variant selector, confirm that changing the selector changes the variant attached to the form.</p><p>Deduplicate repeated requests without erasing the original queue timestamp. A migration should retain variant mappings, suppression records and outstanding requests. A product title is a weak join key when merchandising renames items or reuses a title.</p>
<h2>Check sellable stock rather than a warehouse receipt</h2><p>Decide which inventory locations can fulfill online orders, how allocated stock is treated and what happens when inventory continues selling during a batch. An inventory increase alone is not a guarantee that the advertised variant can be purchased from the customer's destination. Do not open the whole queue while those conditions remain unknown.</p><p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115003872251">Klaviyo's back-in-stock documentation</a> describes a subscription event, an inventory wait and configurable minimum-inventory and notification rules. It also notes physical-store inventory considerations for Shopify POS users. This is a documented workflow to evaluate, not a claim that every app below provides equivalent controls or reserves stock.</p>
<h2>Release a partial restock fairly</h2><p>Choose and disclose a queue rule, such as signup order or a clearly described early-access group. Start with a batch sized to sellable units and expected demand, then recheck stock before releasing the next batch. There is no universally correct stock threshold or alert interval. A small batch limits the number of people told to buy an item that has already sold out.</p><p>Pause remaining sends after sellout and return unserved subscribers to the waiting state according to the stated policy. A click does not prove a purchase. Confirm an order for the matching variant before marking a request fulfilled, and decide whether a later return should reopen that request.</p>
<h2>Three proposed catalog cases</h2><ul><li><strong>Apparel:</strong> restocking XS must not release M subscribers. Test mixed size availability and a selector change before form submission.</li><li><strong>Beauty:</strong> retain shade identity and imagery through catalog updates. A different shade is a recommendation, not fulfillment of the original request.</li><li><strong>Limited collaboration:</strong> explain that alerts do not reserve units. If access is reserved, test the allocation and expiry behavior before mentioning a purchase window.</li></ul>
<h2>Handle discontinued products and preorders</h2><p>When the original variant will not return, retire its waiting state deliberately. Offer alternatives only in a channel and purpose the customer permitted. Do not present an alternative as a restock. When changing to preorder, state delivery expectations and payment terms rather than retaining a misleading available-now alert.</p><p>Connect unavailable-item handling from <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a> to a fresh restock request. Coordinate with <a href="/use-cases/product-launch">product launches</a> so waitlist alerts and general launches do not announce conflicting access rules.</p>
<h2>Proposed checks before live release</h2><p>Use controlled requests for two variants, duplicate signup, withdrawn permission, partial inventory, sellout between batches and a discontinued SKU. Expected results: exact variant matching, one queue entry, suppressed sends, paced release, cancellation and a clear closure path. These checks have not been run by this directory.</p><p>Review false-availability reports, request-to-order rate, unserved queue age and time between stock confirmation and release. Segment those metrics by variant and channel. Separate inventory lag from delivery lag; sending faster cannot fix an incorrect stock source.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration brief</h2><p>The catalog below preserves routes for comparison; it is not a ranking or confirmation that each app supports this workflow or native SMS. For this use case, ask each supplier to demonstrate the state transitions described above, the required integration and plan, export fields, failure handling and the full messaging quote. Include Sequenzy in that evaluation only where its documented channel and integration scope meets your requirements.</p><ul>{candidates.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul><p>For permission and recipient timing design, use the <a href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours</a> guides. This workflow plan does not establish legal applicability or guarantee delivery.</p></article></section></BaseLayout>
